     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my ZZ plant leaves turning yellow, and how do I save it from rhizome rot?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yellow ZZ plant leaves with soft stems at the base are the usual sign of overwatering. When the soil stays wet, the rhizomes start to rot and can no longer feed the leaves."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What are the white, pale or brown patches on my ZZ plant leaves?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Whitish, pale or brown patches on ZZ plant leaves, sometimes with curling, are sun scald from direct sun. In the UAE this often happens behind west- or south-facing glass, or after a move to a sunnier spot."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Are black spots on ZZ plant stems a problem?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Dark spots on firm ZZ plant stems are normal. Clemson Extension notes that a stem with these spots is a typical, healthy ZZ plant."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What are the bumps or white fluff on my ZZ plant?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Small brown bumps on the stems and leaves of a ZZ plant are usually scale insects; white, cottony clusters are mealybugs. ZZ plants have few pest problems, but they can get scale, mealybugs, aphids, thrips and whiteflies."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my ZZ plant not growing?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "ZZ plants grow very slowly and in spurts (Iowa State Extension), and much more slowly in low light (UConn) or in winter. As long as the stems are firm and the leaves are green, a long pause is normal."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can a ZZ plant live in a room with no window?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes, a ZZ plant can live in a room with no window if the room has electric light for several hours each day. UConn notes that a ZZ plant suits even a windowless office if it gets fluorescent light for several hours a day, and NC State notes that it grows very slowly in such low light."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does a ZZ plant clean the air?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ZZ plant does not noticeably clean the air in a normal room. Studies that found plants removing chemicals used small sealed chambers, and a 2019 review found that a building would need 10 to 1,000 plants per square metre of floor to match normal ventilation. Buy a ZZ plant because it looks good and is easy to keep."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can a ZZ plant live outside in the UAE?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Treat a ZZ plant as an indoor plant in the UAE. UConn gives a range of about 13–27 °C (55–80 °F) and Clemson says to keep it above 60 °F (about 16 °C). UAE summer days and nights are hotter than that range, and inland winter nights can be colder. Direct sun also scalds the leaves."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is it safe to touch a ZZ plant?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes, a ZZ plant is safe to touch. NC State Extension lists the ZZ plant as toxic if eaten, but not as a cause of skin rashes. Keep it where pets and young children cannot chew the leaves."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How long does a ZZ plant leaf cutting take to grow?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ZZ plant leaf cutting takes a long time to grow. UConn reports that small rhizomes form at the base of a leaf in about a month in a greenhouse, but indoors it may take up to nine months. Illinois Extension notes that roots come first, then a small rhizome."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How fast does a ZZ plant grow?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ZZ plant grows slowly. Extension services describe the ZZ plant as slow-growing; Iowa State notes that it grows in spurts, and UConn that it grows much more slowly in low light. Over time, most plants reach about 60–120 cm."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does a ZZ plant flower?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A ZZ plant flowers rarely. A ZZ plant flower is a cream or brown spike (spadix) inside a light green hood (spathe), on a short stalk low down among the bases of the leaf stems. UConn notes that flowers sometimes appear in late summer."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is a ZZ plant good for an office?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. A ZZ plant tolerates low light, office lighting and missed waterings, and it is one of the most common plants in commercial interiors. Keep it out of the direct airflow from AC vents."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How is plant size measured?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Size is measured from the bottom of the nursery pot to the top tip of the highest leaf. A plant listed as 50-60 CM stands in that range measured this way."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Which pot option should I choose, nursery or decorative?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Choose the nursery pot for the lowest price or if you already have a planter. Choose a decorative pot for a ready-to-display plant that arrives fully potted with soil mix, perlite and a drainage layer. The plant itself is the same."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do the decorative pots have drainage holes?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Decorative pot orders include a drainage layer at the base, with premium soil mix and perlite. For specific details on a particular pot model, send us the product link and our team will confirm."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is the plant healthy on arrival? Can I check first?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Plants are guaranteed to arrive healthy. Check your plant with the delivery agent: if it's damaged or not what you ordered, you can refuse it on the spot. If you find damage after unpacking, send photos and your order number on WhatsApp (+971 4 294 1422) or email sales@plantsworld.ae within 24 hours and we'll replace it free. You can also ask for a pre-dispatch photo."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Will my plant be safe in the UAE heat during delivery?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. Our own delivery team brings your order, not a third-party courier. In extreme summer heat (June to September) we may move deliveries to farther emirates to a cooler day and agree the new day with you. If anything arrives damaged, we replace it free."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "My plant arrived with a few drooping or yellow leaves. Is that normal?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes, that's normal. A few drooping, bent or yellow leaves are a common reaction to the journey and a new spot, and plants usually recover within a few days. Keep it in bright indirect light and check the soil before watering. If the plant or pot is actually damaged, send photos within 24 hours."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do I need to repot my plant when it arrives?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Not immediately. Let it settle for a few weeks first, and note that a repotted plant can't be returned or replaced, so check it's healthy before you repot. Plants in decorative pots arrive fully potted. Repot only when roots fill the pot or growth stops."
     }
    }
